The Parliamentary Debate competition assesses the competitors’ ability to shape their views and defend them in a structured manner. In oither words, competitors are expected to present coherent arguments for or against a certain prompt within the given timeframe. The selected debate topics centre on common discourse within Canadian politics, philosophical debates relative to the field of Engineering and light-hearted icebreaker-style prompts.     
Roles and Rules
Teams
The debate is between two teams, each with two members. The government will be given the task of presenting an initial set of ideas and arguing their validity. The opposition will then proceed to show how these ideas are undesirable. Before the resolution is revealed, a coin toss is held. The team that wins the toss gets to pick the side that they want to represent. Teams are always expected to act responsibly. Inappropriate language or offensiveness towards the other team, the officials, or the audience is not acceptable. Teams are expected to direct all their speeches towards the Chair.
Resolutions
The resolutions will be assigned by the organising committee and will be validated by the Debate Chairs before the competition begins. They will be related to an issue that the average engineering student should have a defensible opinion on without any preparation. The resolutions will not be truisms. Absolute words such as "all," "everyone," and "always" will also be avoided (e.g. "Be it resolved that all engineers are good at math" is not a good resolution, since there are always exceptions). Once both teams are informed of the resolution, they are given fifteen (15) minutes to prepare for the debate.
Time Allotted to Debaters
The speaking order and times will be as follows:
* First speaker of the government: five (5) minutes
* First speaker of the opposition: five (5) minutes
* Second speaker of the government: five (5) minutes
* Second speaker of the opposition: five (5) minutes
* Rebuttal by the first speaker of the opposition: two (2) minutes
* Rebuttal by the first speaker of the government: two (2) minutes


If a debater is over the time limit, the Chair will allow a fifteen (15) second grace period. Upon the exhaustion of speaking time, loud pounding on tables will be deemed appropriate.
Role of the Government
The government must narrow down the resolution so that the debate will be about one major topic. The resolutions must not be squirrelled or converted into truisms. Squirrelling is the act of redefining the resolution so that it has a meaning different from the one intended by the resolution. The government must also avoid specific knowledge debates where the average engineer has no familiarity with the topic. The government can follow one of two different strategies in the debate
The Principle Case (This House Believes That)
In a principle case, the government presents a principle and a contention. The principle is a general statement that is debatable based on facts, experience, or morals. Examples include "Engineers need to be well rounded" and "Canadians abuse the free health-care system." The contention is the application of the principle to a situation relating to the resolution. The contention must be about one major topic and may be a restatement of the resolution. Example contentions for the above principles would be "Engineering students should be required to take more complementary studies classes" and "People should be discouraged from going to a doctor unless necessary."
The Plan Case (This House Would)
In a plan case, the government still identifies a principle and a contention. Once this is done, they present a plan for implementing the contention and changing the status quo. They must identify the need for change and how the plan will induce this change. Example plans for the above contentions would be “Students should be required to take two years of general studies before entering an engineering program” and “Individuals should be charged a fee every time they visit a doctor.” 


The plain case is the most effective when the principle and contention are almost non-debatable. For example, if the resolution is “Be it resolved that waste management should become more stringent,” the contention is almost unquestionable. However, the proposition can intensify the debate by adding, “Thus, we propose that any household that produces more than a certain quota of waste be severely fined.” In a plan case, it is important that the plan does not become too specific since preparation time is limited and the technical knowledge necessary to develop the plan is non-existent.
The First Speaker
The first speaker of the government must explain the interpretation of the resolution, clearly state the principle and contention, and clarify any definitions. If a plan case is introduced, the entire plan must be outlined in the first speaker's speech. Finally, the first speaker must initiate the argumentation for the contention and plan.
The Second Speaker
The second speaker of the government continues the argumentation of the first speaker and reaffirms concepts that have been attacked by the opposition. New parts of a plan may not be introduced and terms in the resolution may not be defined. New lines of argumentation and new evidence, however, may be introduced. 
Role of the Opposition 
The opposition’s task is to convince the judges that the government’s views are fallacious. If the case presented by the government is a truism or requires specific knowledge to debate, the opposition can point this out in their first speech and will be rewarded accordingly by the judges. If the point is well taken, then the opposition must redefine the resolution in a debatable manner. If the government presents a principle case, the opposition is left with little more to do than attack the principle. If the government presents a plan case, the opposition can attack the principle, the contention or the arguments for the plan. The opposition can also show how the plan will not work or identify the undesirable side effects that it will create. Finally, the opposition may propose a counter plan that is more effective that the original plan. The first speaker of the opposition must introduce counter plans
Guidelines for Defining the Resolution 
As the debate is opened, the Prime Minister must define the resolution and state the debate contention. This must all occur during the opening speech. The rules of the resolution are as follows: 
* The resolution must be debatable. The resolution defined by the Prime Minister may not be truistic. A truism is a statement that is generally accepted to be true and does not contribute any new ideas or information. (i.e. A journey of a thousand miles begins with a single step.) 
*  The resolution definition must be fair. As the Government speaks first, the definition of the resolution is allowed to put the Government at a disadvantage but not the Opposition. 
* The resolution must not put the Opposition's rebuttal in an immoral position. For example, the definition “Community safety should be a priority for all Engineers when considering a system design” would cause the Opposition to take the stance that user safety is not important, which would be considered an immoral position by most.
* The resolution must be interpreted literally by all (no squirreling). The topic cannot be open to vast interpretation. 
* The resolution must be defined in an appropriate manner that is tasteful for all. This will be determined at the discretion of the debaters. If the Debate Moderator does not approve of the definition, they may intervene and penalize the Government.
*  The resolution definition must be based on the present. The setting of the debate cannot be depicted in another time or location.
 The Opposition has the right to redefine the resolution if the guidelines stated above are not met by the Government. This can be redefined in a debatable manner during the opening statements.
 References Permitted 
Competitors are allowed to utilize shared notes for the debate. After the preparation period, competitors are only allowed their shared notes, and may not speak to each other or use any other resources for the remainder of the debate. Competitors are not allowed to use the internet for research during the preparation time. Since we cannot actively monitor this, judges will be reminded to place a greater emphasis on the logic and articulation of the arguments rather than specific supporting evidence that isn’t common to reasonably informed individuals. Specific statistics such as values and dates are not considered common knowledge. 
Rebuttals
During the rebuttals, excluding the first rebuttal of the opposition, no new arguments may be introduced. Debaters are permitted to present new knowledge if, and only if, it supports a previously raised point or counters a point raised by the opposing team. 
 Questions 
Questions will be allowed in this competition. The opponent who wishes to ask a question indicates their desire by standing up and optionally stating “question” or “POI.” If the debater does not wish to take the question, they may indicate so by a simple "no, thank you” or wave them down. If the current debater wishes to entertain the question, it must be stated by the opponent in less than fifteen (15) seconds during which the speaker must yield the floor. Both team members may take part in answering the question. The time used to ask and answer the question comes out of the current debater's allotted time. Each debater must accept one question during his or her speech if the opponent is making an honest attempt to ask a question. Three attempts at asking a question in one speech constitute an honest attempt. Questions will not be allowed in the first or last 30 seconds of a speech, or during rebuttals.
Heckling
 Heckling is permitted within reason if it is brief and witty. The chair may intervene if they believe the situation needs to be controlled and the heckling is distracting from the debate content. Speeches from the floor will not be permitted during the preliminary and semi-final rounds but will be after the final round once the judges have exited to discuss and determine the winner. Further information regarding rebuttals, references, and heckling can be found in the OEC 2026-2027 Official Rulebook.


 Points of Procedure 
Points of Procedure may be raised if a debater believes a rule has been broken. This must be done so by immediately alerting the Debate Moderator of the violation by stating, “Point of Procedure.” The Moderator will then respond with, “Make your point.” When a Point is raised, the Timekeeper will stop the clock, the current debate presenter will sit down, and the individual raising the Point of Procedure will explain how the debate rules have not been followed. The Moderator will respond to this with one of the following: “Point well taken”, “Point not well taken”, or “Point taken under advisement.” The latter response by the speaker leaves the point up for interpretation by the judges to decide if the point is valid or not. The Speaker may also inform the judges of the severity of the point raised for marking purposes. If excessive Points of Procedures have been raised by a team for minuscule issues, the team will be penalised at the speaker’s discretion. Violations that warrant a point of procedure include the following: 
* Unprofessional behaviour 
* Offensive behaviour 
* Misquotations 
* Speaking to the opposition instead of the audience 
* Presenting a new argument in the rebuttal
*  Introduction of parts of a plan by the second speaker


 (*If the government has presented a truism or specific knowledge case, the opposition must wait until the end of the first debater’s speech to point this out.) Moreover, if the officials have failed to follow the rules of debate (incorrect speaking order, too much speaking time allowed, etc.), it may be politely pointed out to them with a Point of Procedure. Points of procedure are commonly seen to detract from a debate when offered incorrectly. Points of procedure that take away from the flow of a debate unnecessarily will be penalised by the judges.
Format 
Presentation Order
The debate schedule will be prepared at random and presented at the pre-competition briefing (at least thirty (30) minutes prior to the first debate). This schedule will remain visible and updated systematically throughout the competition. The schedule should also illustrate the path which a team will follow depending on wins and losses.
Single Elimination
The debate competition will follow a Single Elimination format which ensures all teams will get to participate in at least one (1) debate. The competition begins with matches between randomly assigned pairs of teams.  The winner of each round will progress further into the competition while each losing team will be eliminated  (Note, in the case of an odd number of teams, all but one randomly selected team will debate before the second round commences). Once two teams remain, a final match will be held to determine the top 2 placements. The third and fourth place placements will be determined by the highest score of the two losing semi-finalist teams.  


Teams may have to debate two (2) or more times in a row, due to the dynamic nature of the format, however the Debate Competition Lead should do their best to ensure teams are given reasonable breaks between debates.


