General Tips for Participants
Any successful speech will perform the role outlined above, but also be engaging, interesting and focused. The tips below are provided to help improve speeches




Case Construction
Many novices often commit the same mistakes. This is particularly true in presenting constructive material. Below are three very common mistakes that should be avoided when presenting constructive material. For all of them, consider the resolution “This house supports the outing of closeted homosexual politicians by the LGBT”.


Examples are not Arguments
Many novice debaters try to make arguments by simply giving examples from their side of the house. For instance, a Government debater might say “Aaron Schock was outed and that was a good thing”. While this is arguably true, it is not, itself, an argument. An argument that might be based off of or use this example might
be that “Outing closeted homosexual politicians forces socially conservative parties to engage with homosexuality. This is good because many conservative parties often refuse or deny homosexuality’s existence. By having outed members, this tactic no longer works. See, for example, Aaron Schock from Illinois”. Obviously, this argument can be taken further, but notice the difference between simply stating the example, and explaining what that example has led to.


Titles are not Arguments
Similarly, many novice debaters present arguments by simply asserting their truth. An Opposition team in this debate may state “Being a politician does not deny you the same right to privacy that would be granted to any other person”. While this is a very clear stance, and has some meaning on its own, it forces the question “What right, if any, do we have to privacy?” Here, the Opposition team asserts something without backing it
up. They could progress this idea by saying “Politicians are people too. They chose a job that happens to be more public. However, being in the public sphere does not negate that sense of privacy just like doing any other job would not violate this. Further, this privacy is a means of protection. Insofar as these politicians would very likely be harmed because of their sexuality, we would be violating their ability to protect themselves.” Again, there is a difference between simply asserting that the title of an argument is true and explaining why that might be the case.


Linking
The final common mistake is forgetting to link an argument to the resolution. In general, any argument requires some sort of context that leads to or is a result of the resolution. This context is how an argument is ‘linked’ to the resolution. It is important to provide this link between resolution and argument otherwise an otherwise excellent argument could be misinterpreted as ineffectual or as irrelevant given the context of the
round. For example, Government may argue “In anti-gay states, more LGBT youth are homeless when compared to the national average. We, as Government, seek to reduce this homelessness.” Even with a better explanation of how LGBT youth become homeless at a higher rate, there does not seem to be an inherent link between the resolution and the argument. Thus, this argument could fall out of the round. However, if Government were to then state “by outing LGBT politicians, a greater degree of transparency surrounding the
treatment of LGBT youth would occur, resulting in a lower number of homeless LGBT youth”, they would effectively provide the context in which outing queer politicians would lead to lower homeless youth. Thus, the argument becomes more relevant.




Any excellent constructive argument will avoid the common pitfalls outlined above.


Refutation
Refutation is normally the most understood part of debating; there are not many pitfalls that novice debaters experience. The main problem with refutation is organization. Be very clear about what exactly you are refuting when you do a refutation. For example, a good way to structure your refutation would be “The Opposition said _______, this is incorrect because of ________”. In this way, your refutation becomes more relevant to the round and thus you gain more credit for presenting it. Otherwise, the relevance may be lost and judges may not understand where your criticism is directed.


Examples of Modelling
Consider the resolution “This house regrets gay culture”. What would a good model here be? Recall that any model must be fair, clear and contentious. To make it fair, consider what actors hold a stake in the existence of gay culture. Well, gay activists may be one such actor. Another might be the gay community. So the ‘house’ might be either one of these. By defining the ‘house’ as either gay rights activists or the general gay community, it provides a fair ground to debate on. However, there’s still some ambiguity which violates our
‘models must be clear’ rule. What is meant by ‘gay culture’? Well, ‘gay culture’ could be defined as gay culture in major urban environments. That certainly is clearer than simply ‘gay culture’. Finally, is this model contentious? Is there a tautology? No. Is there something inherently immoral about the stance Opposition must now argue? No. So, this model for the resolution “This house regrets gay culture” would be an adequate one. 


Consider another example “This house would let Greece default”. Again, who would the actors here be? Germany, the IMF or any of the other Troika lenders might be the ‘house’. Greece itself could choose to default and so could be considered the ‘house’. All of these are fair definitions of house, in which those actors have an inherent risk associated with either side of the debate. Is there anything else that needs to be defined other than the actor allowing Greece to default? You could define ‘default’ although it already has a fairly standard definition. Here, simply providing an actor is enough of a model to provide a fair, clear, contentious
debate. Hopefully, these examples help you understand modelling a debate as Government. If you’re stuck, remember to think of who is acting in any given debate and to clarify any ambiguity in the resolution. Usually, those two things are enough to provide a good model.
Points of Clarification
Points of Clarification are a way to ask a question about the model that is unclear. There are no ways for the Opposition to insert arguments in the round and should be as neutral as possible. They may only be offered by an Opposition speaker since Government should fully understand their model. They may only be asked in the
Prime Minister’s speech. To give one as an Opposition speaker, stand up, say ‘Clarification’ and wait for the Prime Minister to take you. The Prime Minister is obligated to take all Points of Clarification. 


This is because any ambiguity in the Government model often leads to an unclear, poor round of debate. Opposition teams should not abuse their ability to ask Points of Clarification. Asking questions that the judge deems unnecessary in the debate or using Points of Clarification in excess to the detriment of the debate will be punished.
Points of Information
Points of Information are short questions or statements that can be used by debaters to interject information into another’s speech. To offer one, simply stand up with an arm extended. Do not say anything. If it is not a Point of Clarification, it is assumed to be a point of information. 


While giving a speech, if someone offers a Point of Information, you are not obligated to take it. You can wave them down, respond by saying something along the lines of “not at this time” or dismiss them in another way. However, it is expected at this tournament
that each team takes at least two Points of Information each round. One speaker may handle both Points of Information and the other none, but this is the expectation. Not taking Points of Information even if the other team is offering them continuously will be punished.


Note: Points of Information must be short. They should absolutely not exceed 10-15 seconds in length and speakers accepting the Point of Information are encouraged to wave down the person giving them a Point of Information if they have exceeded the 15 second time limit.




Heckling
Heckling is permitted at this tournament and should be used for a laugh. Excessive or entirely disruptive heckling will be punished by judges. Again, like Points of Information, heckles should be incredibly short, not exceeding a few seconds. Longer than this and the heckles often become detrimental to the round. Contentious ideas or more nuanced analysis should be left for your respective speeches or a Point of Information.
Evaluation 
Judging
The debate is judged based upon the most convincing argument,communication skills, and ability to follow outlined procedures. Each judge will decide individually, and the moderator will tally the decisions and announce the winner of each round.


